{"author":{"id":"dec876858d28babbadcead4137861bf586626b8518cbce9c5f4fc52ac3b424b8"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1795858730,"nonce":"aDZSD+LZ3fFa9kz9u2/bRYybPdA=","title":"blinc_gpu: two parity tests skip their pixel checks on a GL adapter","message":"`mesh_parity::the_scene_keeps_its_pinned_pixels` returns early on a GL adapter, and `path_parity` skips its gradient ramp assertions there (`parity_harness::on_gl`).\n\nCI's Coverage job runs a software GL adapter. It shades a shadowed pixel further from the Metal and Vulkan pins than the tolerance allows, and it read the path shader's 1D gradient ramp as black. A `WGPU_BACKEND=gl` run on llvmpipe on the Linux box matched both, so the difference is in the Coverage environment and the cause is not known.\n\nA GL device is the nearest thing here to WebGL2, so a fault in either path on GL would pass unseen.\n\nSplit from d35bfb3.","files":null}]}